# What the owner reviews, and nothing else. A path is the owner's when a change to it could do harm
# that no later check of the owner's would catch: it moves a secret or a key, changes who may log in
# or what runs as root, reaches the mail host, creates or changes a billed resource, changes what
# the public can reach, shortens a database backup, or weakens a rule, a gate or an exemption that
# judges a merge or a deploy. Every other path merges on the reviewer App's approval, and a host
# configuration change that is merged still reaches a host only through the gated apply.
# The last matching line wins and there is no catch-all line. A line with no owner un-owns what it
# matches, so the released paths below come after the directories they are cut out of, and the
# files that must stay the owner's are listed again after them. The shared standards lines are
# last (SYNC-1). A directory that is owned below and not released stays owned for every new file in
# it, so a new file of a kept kind fails closed. ansible/checks/test_codeowners.py proves this file
# against sample paths and against the paths each kept class names.

